\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A time map without a spin grading}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On the same periodic grid let a conjugate-linear isometric
bijection #{c_\eta} on spin satisfy
#{c_\eta\varepsilon_j c_\eta^{-1}=\eta\varepsilon_j} for
every #{j}, with a common sign #{\eta=\pm1}. Assume the same
self-adjoint Clifford directions as in the
\vocabk{spinor time map}{fdip-017Z}, but allow any spin space
with these relations, with or without a grading. Take any
fiber antiunitary #{c_F:F\to F}. The map
##{(S'_t\psi)(x)=
  \bigl((\varepsilon_t c_\eta)\otimes c_F\bigr)
   \psi(\theta_s x)}
is antiunitary, with square #{\eta c_\eta^2\otimes c_F^2}
on values. For the links #{U'} of \ref{fdip-0182} now use
#{u\mapsto c_Fu c_F^{-1}} in both reflected-link formulas.
In this convention
##{\begin{aligned}
 S'_t D_{a,U}(S'_t)^{-1}&=-\eta D_{a,U'},\\
 S'_t L_{a,U}(S'_t)^{-1}&=L_{a,U'}.
\end{aligned}}
For #{\eta=-1} this also carries #{D_{W,a,U}} to
#{D_{W,a,U'}} with the same real coefficient.}

\proof{
\p{The two antiunitary factors use the same conjugation of
scalars, so their tensor action is antiunitary by the pure-tensor
argument of \ref{fdip-0181}. The coordinate pullback commutes
with them. Squaring the spin factor gives
#{\varepsilon_t(c_\eta\varepsilon_t c_\eta^{-1})c_\eta^2
=\eta c_\eta^2}. The fiber contributes #{c_F^2}.}

\p{On spin, conjugation by #{\varepsilon_t c_\eta} gives sign
#{\eta} on the time direction and #{-\eta} on each spatial
direction. The spatial shifts retain their directions, while
time pullback takes the time shift to its inverse with the
shifted positive link of \ref{fdip-0182}. Here the action on
a link is forward conjugation by #{c_F}. Consequently each
centered summand has sign #{-\eta}, and the Laplacian has
sign plus. For real #{c_W} the Wilson image is
#{-\eta D_{a,U'}-c_W L_{a,U'}}. This gives the asserted
same-coefficient identity on the negative-sign branch.}
}
